---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import GifAudioToMp3 from '@/components/tools/media/GifAudioToMp3.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="flac-to-mp3">
  <GifAudioToMp3
    client:load
    accept=".flac,.oga,audio/flac"
    sourceLabel="FLAC"
    sourceHint="Drop a whole album: 20 tracks or 300 MB at once"
    target="mp3"
    defaultBitrate="320"
    formatNote="FLAC is lossless, so there is no generation loss on the way in. The MP3 bitrate is purely your own quality-versus-space decision."
  />
  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>The one conversion where bitrate is entirely your choice</h2>
    <p>
      Most lossy-to-lossy conversions limit you: below the source bitrate, artefacts add up; above it, you
      waste space on information already discarded. FLAC is lossless: decoded, it gives back the original
      samples bit for bit, so the MP3 encoder starts from a perfect master. The bitrate you choose is the
      only lossy step in the chain.
    </p>

    <h2>What each setting costs and saves</h2>
    <p>
      The figures are for a five-minute stereo track ripped from CD, which is about 50 MB as WAV and 30 MB as
      FLAC.
    </p>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>MP3 setting</th><th>Track size</th><th>12-track album</th><th>Verdict</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>320 kbps</td><td>≈ 12 MB</td><td>≈ 144 MB</td><td>Sounds identical to the source for almost everyone, on almost any system</td></tr>
          <tr><td>256 kbps</td><td>≈ 9.6 MB</td><td>≈ 115 MB</td><td>Very close; 20% smaller, and few people can hear the difference</td></tr>
          <tr><td>192 kbps</td><td>≈ 7.2 MB</td><td>≈ 86 MB</td><td>The sensible default for phones and cars</td></tr>
          <tr><td>128 kbps</td><td>≈ 4.8 MB</td><td>≈ 58 MB</td><td>Audible on cymbals and applause through good headphones</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      The table stops at 320 kbps, because that is the maximum of the MP3 format. For better quality than
      320 kbps MP3, keep the FLAC.
    </p>

    <h2>How much does FLAC actually compress?</h2>
    <p>
      FLAC uses linear prediction and Rice coding: it predicts each sample from the samples before it and
      stores only the difference. On typical CD-rate music, this gives 55–65% of the WAV size with no loss.
      Dense, loud, heavily limited modern masters compress worst (about 70%); sparse acoustic recordings and
      classical music with real dynamic range compress best (sometimes under 50%). The compression level a
      ripper used does not change the decoded audio; it only changed the encoding time.
    </p>

    <h2>Why people convert their FLAC library</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Phone storage: a 400 GB FLAC library becomes 90 GB at 192 kbps, so it can fit on the device.</li>
      <li>Car head units: many read only MP3 from USB, or read FLAC only from some filesystems.</li>
      <li>DJ software and gym equipment: both support few formats.</li>
      <li>Sharing: a 30 MB track is awkward to send; a 7 MB one is not.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Keep the FLAC as your archive and treat the MP3s as a copy you can make again at any time. Then a
      future device with more storage, or a better codec, costs you nothing.
    </p>

    <h2>Tags and album art</h2>
    <p>
      FLAC uses Vorbis comments plus a separate picture block. FFmpeg maps the standard fields onto ID3v2.3,
      so title, artist, album, album artist, date, track number, disc number and genre are kept. Embedded
      cover art usually survives; very large images, unusual picture types such as "back cover" or "artist
      photo", and multiple images per file may not. Fields that exist only in Vorbis comments (MusicBrainz
      identifiers, ReplayGain values, custom keys) have no ID3v2.3 equivalent and are dropped.
    </p>

    <h2>Converting an entire album locally</h2>
    <p>
      Select all tracks at once. Up to fifty files go into the queue and convert one after another, each
      with its own progress row, and you can download the set as one ZIP. A whole album takes less than a
      minute on a modern laptop.
    </p>
    <p>
      Upload converters often limit free use to two files an hour or 100 MB per upload, which does not fit
      an album of twenty files and 300 MB. Here your processor does the encoding, so the only limit is your
      device, and your library stays on it.
    </p>

    <h2>Related tools</h2>
    <p>
      For an uncompressed master for an editor or sampler, use <a href="/mp3-to-wav">MP3 to WAV</a> or open
      the FLAC in a desktop editor. To compress large recordings you made yourself, use
      <a href="/wav-to-mp3">WAV to MP3</a>. The <a href="/audio-converter">general audio converter</a>
      handles all other format pairs.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
